ECSC – Elliptic Curve Log Property (crypto)

Category: Crypto Points: TBD Flag: ECSC{l0g_pr0perty_w0rks_d1scr3t3ly_9d03dde5}

Challenge

The server generates a random elliptic curve E over F_p, with a base point G whose order factors into 9 primes of roughly 34–35 bits each — small enough for Pohlig–Hellman to be tractable.

from Crypto.Util.number import bytes_to_long

FLAG = bytes_to_long(open("flag.txt", "rb").read().strip()[len("ECSC{"):-1])
proof.arithmetic(False)

p = 1789850433742566803999659961102071018708588095996784752439608585988988036381340404632423562593
a = 62150203092456938230366891668382702110196631396589305390157506915312399058961554609342345998
b = 1005820216843804918712728918305396768000492821656453232969553225956348680715987662653812284211
F = GF(p)
E.<G> = EllipticCurve(F, [a, b])

assert FLAG < G.order()

k = randrange(G.order())
P = k * G
Q = FLAG * P

res = []
for _ in range(42):
    a = randrange(G.order())
    b = randrange(G.order())
    res.append((a, b, a * P + b * Q))

print(res)




The output is a list of 42 tuples (a_i, b_i, R_i) with

R_i = a_i * P + b_i * Q

Note that neither P nor Q (nor G) is ever leaked directly — we only see random linear combinations of them.

The order of G factors as:

12775224751 · 13026062843 · 18511195699 · 24508446437 · 25961704469 ·
28450356619 · 31034521019 · 31982226581 · 32337773063

(9 primes, each ≈ 2^34–2^35, product N ≈ 300 bits).

Vulnerability
Smooth group order. ord(G) = N is a product of small primes, so the discrete log problem on any point in ⟨G⟩ can be solved efficiently via Pohlig–Hellman (baby-step giant-step / Pollard's rho per prime factor, then CRT).
Linear relation without needing G. Since P = k·G and Q = FLAG·k·G, every published point can be written as
   R_i = a_i·P + b_i·Q = k·(a_i + b_i·FLAG)·G

So every R_i is just some scalar multiple of G — but crucially, that scalar multiple is also a multiple of any other R_j, because they all live in the same cyclic subgroup generated by G (with overwhelming probability the random coefficients a_j + b_j·FLAG and k are coprime to N, so any R_j itself generates the full order-N subgroup).

This means we never need to recover P, Q, or G at all. We can pick any two of the leaked points, e.g. R_0 and R_1, and compute the discrete log of one with respect to the other:

   t = dlog_{R0}(R1)      i.e. the k such that   k · R0 = R1

Only one Pohlig–Hellman computation is needed (not two), because the unknown FLAG then falls out of a single linear equation.

Solving for the flag

Let (a1, b1, R0) = res[0] and (a2, b2, R1) = res[1].

Write c0 = a1 + b1·FLAG and c1 = a2 + b2·FLAG (mod N), so that R0 = k_secret·c0·G and R1 = k_secret·c1·G.

Computing t such that t · R0 = R1 gives (after cancelling the shared k_secret factor, which is invertible mod N with overwhelming probability):

t · c0 ≡ c1   (mod N)
t · (a1 + b1·FLAG) ≡ a2 + b2·FLAG   (mod N)

Solving for FLAG:

FLAG = (a2 − t·a1) · (t·b1 − b2)^−1   (mod N)

Careful with direction/orientation. Depending on how you call discrete_log (i.e. which point is the "base" and which is the "target"), the roles of (a1,b1) and (a2,b2) swap in the final formula. Always sanity-check with a known value, or derive the formula symbolically before plugging in numbers — it's easy to get a silently-wrong (but plausible-looking) FLAG from a sign/index mixup.
